// probe: n_panic
#pragma plugin nvmidi

// Why this file exists.
//
// n_type.nvgt measured that midi_input and midi_output are the only names of
// this plugin a script cannot declare, while midi_note and midi_config - which
// this plugin registers at the same size 0 - declare without a word. Two
// candidates survive that measurement, and they are indistinguishable from
// outside: the engine may be refusing the registration of those two types, or
// a method further down the registering function may be panicking and taking
// the still-unregistered type with it. A panic prints nothing, and a refusal
// caused by earlier damage prints the same sentence as a refusal caused by the
// declaration itself, so asking for the name again cannot tell them apart.
//
// What separates them is asking for something ELSE that is certainly wrong.
//
// MIDI_PROBE_A declares a handle to the port and then calls open with an
// argument no overload accepts. If the type is usable the engine resolves it,
// walks the overloads, finds none and says so. If the type is damaged the
// engine cannot resolve it at all and says that instead. The wording of the
// refusal is the answer.
//
// MIDI_PROBE_B is the control. An array of the same type is a mistake too, but
// the array is a registered type of its own, so the declaration reaches the
// element type with a compiler that is already past the "is this a type"
// question. If A refuses the type and B does not, the type is fine and the
// declaration in n_type.nvgt was refused for another reason.
void main() {
	print("PANIC_BEGIN");

	print("MIDI_PROBE_A");
	midi_output@ p = null;
	p.open("not a port number");
	print("MIDI_PROBE_A_DONE");

	print("MIDI_PROBE_B");
	midi_output[]@ a;
	print("MIDI_PROBE_B_DONE");

	print("MIDI_PROBE_C");
	midi_input@ q = null;
	q.open("not a port number");
	print("MIDI_PROBE_C_DONE");

	print("PANIC_END");
}
